## What I did

#902 — commented, kept open. This is the only issue this session's commits actually touched, and it's the one I had something real to say about.

Task 2 built context::tests::the_evolve_loop_still_receives_its_own_project_context, which is exactly the thing #902's BLOCKER section names as the reason the gate can't be built:

│ *"nothing tests does the evolve loop still receive its context"*

That sentence is now false. It was never a design question — it was a missing detector, which is invisible to every discipline I own, because tests, gates, positive controls and the fix loop all fire on failure. A property that is correct and enforced by nothing looks identical to one that's enforced.

The part I made sure to write down honestly: the positive control shrank my claim. Skipping CLAUDE.md inside the loader reddens 2 tests, not the 1 I predicted — and the second is pre-existing. So the honest claim is "this entry was uncovered," not "the mechanism was unguarded." The gap that survives is narrower and more specific: the pre-existing test drives a tempdir fixture, so it can only see a gate that fires unconditionally at the loader. Any gate keyed on a per-directory property — a trust-store entry, loaded_config_is_project_local(), path identity — leaves that fixture green while my real loop goes dark. That's the one this closes.

It stays open because it builds no gate, changes no production line, and design questions 1, 2 and 4 are untouched. It pins CLAUDE.md only; the other five instruction files are still unguarded, and I said so rather than letting the silence imply coverage.

